WEBVTT

00:16.640 --> 00:20.000
[theme song playing]

00:55.480 --> 00:58.160
I thought you said
we were going to the fair.

00:59.400 --> 01:02.960
We have business first.
Come on. Bring your wits.

01:03.280 --> 01:05.640
- What business?
- That's the Lee family.

01:06.400 --> 01:08.400
- Tommy!
- Johnny Dogs.

01:08.480 --> 01:10.160
Tommy, how the hell are you?

01:10.240 --> 01:13.600
All the better for getting the city smoke
out of my lungs.

01:13.680 --> 01:16.240
I thought you became
a bit too grand for us.

01:16.320 --> 01:17.400
I've been busy.

01:17.480 --> 01:20.760
- I'm riding with the Lee family now.
- I heard.

01:21.080 --> 01:23.200
Myself, I'd rather live among pigs.

01:23.280 --> 01:27.800
Come on, Tommy. No disputing.
So, your first fair since France?

01:27.920 --> 01:31.840
What do you know about France,
you war-shy Gypsy bastard?

01:33.000 --> 01:36.040
- So this is the horse?
- And that's the car.

01:50.840 --> 01:52.640
[Arthur] Hang on a minute.

01:52.720 --> 01:56.160
You're not swapping the family car
for a bloody horse.

01:56.240 --> 01:58.160
Of course we're not swapping it.

01:59.200 --> 02:02.160
- That would be mad.
- We're going to play two-up.

02:21.960 --> 02:24.280
- Here you are.
- I knew it.

02:24.360 --> 02:26.800
I knew it.
Tommy, you bloody idiot.

02:26.880 --> 02:28.680
Shut up, Arthur, I won.

02:28.760 --> 02:30.400
- Good.
- I promised Johnny,

02:30.480 --> 02:33.160
I'd let him have a spin
in the car if he lost.

02:33.240 --> 02:34.440
All right.

02:34.520 --> 02:36.600
[laughing]

02:39.280 --> 02:41.720
Are you Lee boys
laughing at my brother?

02:44.000 --> 02:45.040
Are you?

02:46.120 --> 02:49.640
- Eh? I asked you a question.
- Tommy, come on!

02:49.720 --> 02:51.400
Come on, it's just a crack.

02:51.480 --> 02:54.680
Get your family out of here
and go and enjoy yourselves

02:54.760 --> 02:56.840
at the fair
before they start a war.

02:56.920 --> 03:02.040
Go on. Go. They're from good people.
Their granddad was a King.

03:02.520 --> 03:04.320
Their granddad was a King.

03:04.840 --> 03:07.320
Yeah, but his mother
was a Diddicoy whore.

04:34.120 --> 04:37.280
We will take them
before last night's beer,

04:37.360 --> 04:40.040
turns to piss and wakes the devils up.

04:40.680 --> 04:42.920
We will leave no stone unturned.

04:43.000 --> 04:47.200
Every gun, every bullet
will be brought to me for inspection.

04:47.280 --> 04:50.240
Now, take your positions.

05:11.680 --> 05:14.720
[whistle blowing]

05:15.360 --> 05:17.800
[people screaming]

05:23.640 --> 05:25.680
[breathing heavily]

05:27.560 --> 05:28.560
Freddie.

05:30.560 --> 05:32.000
[knock at door]

05:32.120 --> 05:33.520
[panting]

05:33.600 --> 05:36.080
You're sure your brothers are at the fair?

05:36.160 --> 05:38.360
If it was them, they wouldn't knock.

05:38.440 --> 05:39.600
[man] Mr. Thorne!

05:40.440 --> 05:41.520
What is it?

05:41.600 --> 05:44.080
The police are rounding up the communists.

05:44.800 --> 05:48.040
Come on, Ada, if the cops
find me we are screwed. Fuck!

05:50.040 --> 05:52.760
- [policeman] Freddie Thorne, open up!
- Ada!

06:01.360 --> 06:04.200
[screaming]

06:11.360 --> 06:14.720
You have to jump, Ada.
I'll catch you. Come on.

06:22.640 --> 06:24.280
Look at this.

06:24.360 --> 06:28.400
A prescription for iron tablets
for Ada fucking Shelby.

06:30.080 --> 06:31.160
[knocking on door]

06:31.400 --> 06:33.320
Mrs. Donovan, I need that favor.

06:33.400 --> 06:36.440
- Come in and be quick.
- I'm not coming in. She is.

06:36.520 --> 06:38.520
Holy Jesus, it's the Shelby girl.

06:38.600 --> 06:40.600
You've not seen her with me, okay?

06:40.680 --> 06:42.680
Just give her a cup of tea and let her
stay until the police have gone.

06:42.760 --> 06:45.680
She's the sister of those
Peaky Blinder devils.

06:45.840 --> 06:48.560
And yet, she is an angel.
Come on, Ada.

06:48.760 --> 06:50.800
What are you going to do, Freddie?

06:50.880 --> 06:53.400
Me? I'm gonna have to
leave town for a bit.

06:56.000 --> 06:58.200
[women shouting]

07:04.200 --> 07:06.800
[door opens]

07:10.760 --> 07:13.080
A gentleman would take off his hat,

07:14.560 --> 07:16.360
and put out his pipe.

07:24.640 --> 07:26.920
I see you Specials
only dare come here,

07:27.000 --> 07:29.600
when you know the boys
are away at the fair.

07:29.680 --> 07:31.320
You mean your nephews?

07:31.720 --> 07:36.200
With their guns and their razors?
Is it them you're lighting candles for?

07:37.280 --> 07:38.320
No.

07:38.400 --> 07:41.480
I am lighting candles
for the boys from the Garrison

07:41.560 --> 07:43.600
who lost their lives in France.

07:44.760 --> 07:46.240
There's a list there.

07:48.200 --> 07:49.280
Look.

07:54.520 --> 07:58.680
I hear you didn't make it to France,
Inspector Campbell.

08:01.840 --> 08:03.520
You've heard of me.

08:04.880 --> 08:06.720
I've heard of you.

08:09.840 --> 08:12.320
Is it the Holy Grail you're looking for?

08:12.440 --> 08:17.160
As a matter of fact,
it is the Holy Grail I'm looking for.

08:17.280 --> 08:20.240
Something precious.
Something stolen.

08:20.680 --> 08:23.000
Perhaps you know
what I'm talking about.

08:25.960 --> 08:30.320
Sorry, I misunderstood your intention
when you pushed me against the wall.

08:42.760 --> 08:45.560
Turn the place upside down.

08:47.840 --> 08:50.360
Arthur Shelby said you people
would help us.

08:50.440 --> 08:53.680
If we don't know what you've lost,
how can we help you find it?

08:53.800 --> 08:56.320
But I have found out subsequently that
I was speaking to the wrong man.

08:56.400 --> 08:59.200
Next time I want to talk to the boss.

09:00.760 --> 09:04.400
Lickey Tea Rooms. Friday, ten o'clock

09:04.480 --> 09:07.200
And when I say, "The boss,"
I mean Thomas.

09:12.640 --> 09:13.840
Come on.

09:39.800 --> 09:42.560
Now, what the bloody hell's
been going on here?

09:44.600 --> 09:45.640
Jesus Christ.

09:45.720 --> 09:47.760
[Polly] The coppers told everyone

09:47.840 --> 09:50.320
Arthur'd agreed to it
when he was arrested.

09:50.400 --> 09:54.760
They said the Peaky Blinders had cleared
out to the fair to let them do it.

09:54.840 --> 09:58.840
I never said nothing to that copper about
smashing up bloody houses.

09:58.920 --> 10:03.120
- All right. Which pubs did they do?
- The Guns, the Chain, the Marquis.

10:03.200 --> 10:05.680
All the ones that pay you
to protect them.

10:05.760 --> 10:08.360
Only one they didn't touch
was the Garrison.

10:08.440 --> 10:11.040
Make sure people think
we were in on it.

10:11.440 --> 10:13.320
He's smart, this copper.

10:14.520 --> 10:16.680
So, go on.
Drink your beers, get out.

10:16.760 --> 10:20.320
You'd better show people you are still
the cocks of the walk.

10:20.400 --> 10:23.200
Hand out some cash
to the landlords of the pubs.

10:23.280 --> 10:25.520
Pay some veterans
to fix the places up.

10:25.600 --> 10:29.200
- [John] Nipper, give us the bag.
- So, what about you, Tommy?

10:29.280 --> 10:32.000
I have to go to Charlie's
to stable the horse.

10:32.080 --> 10:34.000
She looked footsore in the box.

10:37.400 --> 10:39.160
Let them see your faces.

10:47.720 --> 10:50.200
So we both know
what they were looking for.

10:55.840 --> 10:59.280
- You don't read the papers.
- Racing papers.

10:59.560 --> 11:01.560
So let me tell you the odds.

11:01.800 --> 11:04.960
I reckon it's three to one,
there'll be a revolution.

11:05.040 --> 11:08.680
- I wouldn't bet on that.
- That copper's betting on it.

11:08.760 --> 11:12.200
He's not going to let it rest
till he gets those guns back.

11:17.640 --> 11:18.720
[sighing]

11:19.640 --> 11:21.360
Did he talk to you, Pol?

11:22.880 --> 11:24.200
In the church.

11:25.480 --> 11:27.400
Did he try to find our Ada?

11:28.440 --> 11:29.880
She was sleeping.

11:30.360 --> 11:31.440
[scoffing]

11:33.280 --> 11:35.240
Where was she sleeping, Pol?

11:36.760 --> 11:39.480
I thought you didn't care
for women's business.

11:45.360 --> 11:48.920
He knows you're the boss.
He wants to meet you.

11:55.280 --> 11:57.800
- Will you talk to him?
- No.

11:59.240 --> 12:01.960
You don't parley
when you're on the back foot.

12:03.920 --> 12:06.040
We'll strike a blow back first.

12:22.000 --> 12:24.400
Finn, lad?
What are you doing?

12:24.480 --> 12:26.120
We're having a fire.

12:26.200 --> 12:27.600
[knock on door]

12:27.960 --> 12:30.720
- Two bob for your picture of the King.
- What?

12:30.800 --> 12:32.640
Bring it to Watery Lane.

12:32.760 --> 12:34.760
- Hello, Finn lad.
- Hello, Scud.

12:34.840 --> 12:37.520
[Scudboat] We are blocking
the entrance, mate.

12:39.000 --> 12:40.240
Come on.

12:44.720 --> 12:45.840
[people cheering]

12:54.440 --> 12:55.840
Chuck them on, boys.

12:55.920 --> 12:57.800
[all cheering]

13:01.160 --> 13:03.560
I hope to God you know
what you're doing.

13:23.720 --> 13:27.400
I'm a reporter
with the Birmingham Evening Despatch.

13:27.480 --> 13:29.280
I had a phone call.

13:29.640 --> 13:32.120
Someone called Thomas Shelby?

13:32.200 --> 13:33.560
Come with me.

13:35.440 --> 13:38.040
[Fred] Come on, folks,
give us a bit of room.

13:38.120 --> 13:39.400
Tommy.

13:39.840 --> 13:41.800
Move back for us, please, folks.

13:41.880 --> 13:43.520
- You're Mr. Shelby?
- I am.

13:43.600 --> 13:45.120
All the way back, please.

13:47.640 --> 13:51.040
- You said, I would be protected.
- You're protected.

13:52.200 --> 13:53.720
What's going on?

13:53.880 --> 13:56.720
There are some things
I want you to write down.

13:58.000 --> 13:59.560
Now, first of all,

13:59.640 --> 14:03.040
it's not that people around here
are disloyal to the King.

14:03.120 --> 14:04.240
It's the opposite.

14:04.320 --> 14:07.360
You see, we don't want
our beloved King looking down

14:07.440 --> 14:10.240
and seeing the things
that are being done to us.

14:10.320 --> 14:12.320
So we are taking down
his pictures.

14:12.400 --> 14:14.160
But why are you burning them?

14:14.600 --> 14:17.400
We went through hell for our King.

14:17.480 --> 14:20.880
Walked through the flames of war.
Write all this down.

14:20.960 --> 14:24.200
And now we're being attacked
in our own homes.

14:24.280 --> 14:26.440
These new coppers over from Belfast,

14:26.520 --> 14:29.720
breaking into our homes and interfering
with our women.

14:29.800 --> 14:33.160
We don't think our King would
want to see that happening.

14:33.240 --> 14:36.240
So we're lighting fires
to raise the alarm.

14:36.320 --> 14:39.680
May I ask you, in what capacity
do you speak?

14:39.760 --> 14:41.160
No capacity.

14:41.840 --> 14:46.400
I am an ordinary man.
I won gallantry medals at the Somme.

14:46.800 --> 14:50.040
I want you to write in your paper
what's going on here.

14:51.280 --> 14:52.760
Go on, go.

14:52.840 --> 14:57.280
muttering

14:57.360 --> 15:01.080
muttering

15:01.200 --> 15:02.280
[knock on door]

15:02.360 --> 15:03.440
Come in.

15:03.520 --> 15:04.840
[door opens]

15:06.840 --> 15:11.360
There, there. There's a telephone call
for you, sir, on the London line.

15:11.680 --> 15:14.280
There is?
It's almost midnight.

15:14.360 --> 15:17.120
It's Mr. Winston Churchill, sir.

15:24.080 --> 15:25.480
[door closes]

15:29.840 --> 15:31.760
- Yes, sir?
- Inspector Campbell.

15:31.840 --> 15:34.480
I hear there has been
a bonfire in your city.

15:34.560 --> 15:38.680
There was a small fire reported
at around nine o' clock on Watery Lane.

15:38.760 --> 15:41.320
Certainly nothing to
trouble you about, sir.

15:41.400 --> 15:44.200
But I'm afraid it has troubled me.

15:44.280 --> 15:47.600
Half an hour ago,
I received a wire communication

15:47.680 --> 15:51.480
from the editor
of the Birmingham Evening Despatch.

15:51.680 --> 15:55.360
It seems one of his reporters
was invited to the fire,

15:55.440 --> 15:59.080
and discovered that they were burning
pictures of the King.

15:59.160 --> 16:00.480
Of the King?

16:00.840 --> 16:05.240
Any newspaper report which may cause
embarrassment to His Majesty,

16:05.320 --> 16:09.640
is always referred first and as a matter
of routine to the Home Office.

16:09.720 --> 16:13.120
I will go down there immediately
and arrest those involved.

16:13.200 --> 16:16.800
No, you most certainly
will not make arrests.

16:16.880 --> 16:19.280
If there are arrests
there will be trials,

16:19.360 --> 16:22.800
if there are trials there will be more
newspaper reports,

16:22.880 --> 16:27.560
which will necessarily mention the burning
of the King's likeness.

16:27.640 --> 16:32.240
I put you in Birmingham
for one very specific reason.

16:32.320 --> 16:35.960
Are you any nearer to finding
those stolen guns?

16:36.040 --> 16:39.040
- We are making steady progress.
- We need results.

16:39.120 --> 16:42.840
- Good night, Mr. Campbell.
- Sir. Could I at least ask,

16:42.920 --> 16:46.600
does this report name
any of those involved?

16:46.800 --> 16:52.160
It names a Mr. Thomas Shelby.
The report mentions he is a war hero.

17:02.880 --> 17:04.920
Good of you to join us.

17:05.200 --> 17:07.840
- Where have you been all day?
- [Ada] In bed.

17:08.360 --> 17:12.040
Couldn't sleep.
Then I couldn't wake up.

17:12.120 --> 17:15.320
Then I was cold and then I had
to go for a wee.

17:15.600 --> 17:20.240
Then I was with this bear on a boat,
but that was just a dream.

17:20.320 --> 17:21.640
Then I was hungry.

17:22.880 --> 17:24.720
Why are you reading the paper?

17:25.840 --> 17:26.920
Why wouldn't I be?

17:27.000 --> 17:29.040
I've never seen you
read the paper.

17:29.120 --> 17:31.760
I've only ever seen you
light fires with them.

17:31.840 --> 17:35.440
BSA are on strike.
The miners are on strike.

17:35.520 --> 17:39.000
IRA are killing our boys,
ten a day.

17:42.680 --> 17:43.800
What?

17:44.600 --> 17:46.080
Stand up.

17:46.160 --> 17:48.560
- Why?
- Just stand up.

17:54.920 --> 17:56.120
Side on.

17:58.120 --> 17:59.880
What are you doing?

18:00.240 --> 18:01.360
Ada,

18:03.160 --> 18:04.600
how late are you?

18:07.440 --> 18:08.600
One week.

18:11.120 --> 18:12.800
Five weeks.

18:13.160 --> 18:16.480
Seven if you count weekends.
I think it's a lack of iron.

18:17.240 --> 18:18.720
I got some tablets.

18:19.520 --> 18:20.840
But they didn't work.

18:24.360 --> 18:25.440
No.

18:34.960 --> 18:37.520
[Mrs. Simeon] You can
get dressed now, love.

18:38.120 --> 18:39.880
So, am I or not?

18:45.120 --> 18:48.800
Keep bloody walking, Ada.
If anybody sees us here they'll know.

18:48.880 --> 18:51.040
I'm not getting rid of it, Aunt Pol.

18:51.120 --> 18:53.360
Just come home
and we'll talk about it.

18:53.440 --> 18:55.920
You get off me or I'll scream it.
I swear.

18:58.760 --> 19:02.120
All right, you want to do this
on the street, let's do it.

19:02.200 --> 19:03.440
Whose is it?

19:03.520 --> 19:07.160
If I tell you, you'll tell them
and they'll cut him to pieces.

19:07.520 --> 19:09.520
Not if he marries you, they won't.

19:12.160 --> 19:13.680
Will he marry you?

19:14.080 --> 19:17.000
- I don't know where he is.
- Jesus Christ, Ada!

19:17.080 --> 19:19.560
He's gone away but he said
he'll come back.

19:19.640 --> 19:23.040
- But they all say they'll come back.
- He's not like that.

19:23.120 --> 19:25.040
He's a good man. He promised.

19:25.120 --> 19:28.240
[crying] He will come back, Aunt Pol,
I know he will!

19:36.240 --> 19:38.480
Get me down a Spanish saddle, Curly.

19:38.600 --> 19:40.760
Yes, Tommy, a Spanish saddle.

19:40.840 --> 19:44.440
He's a beauty.
Yeah, yeah, beautiful. Yeah.

19:50.120 --> 19:52.720
[Charlie] A fine beast
you got there, Thomas.

19:54.000 --> 19:57.120
But is he worth falling out
with the Lee family over?

20:08.960 --> 20:11.040
I'm just passing it on.

20:12.000 --> 20:15.240
A declaration of war.
The whole Lee clan.

20:21.080 --> 20:23.080
The guns and now this?

20:25.400 --> 20:28.320
The guns are not spoken of.

20:29.240 --> 20:30.320
Right?

20:33.240 --> 20:34.480
Tommy...

20:34.960 --> 20:36.040
Tommy!

20:38.680 --> 20:42.680
- You mind how you speak to me.
- Forgive me, Uncle.

20:47.520 --> 20:51.000
It's Thomas Shelby against
the whole bloody world, right?

21:15.520 --> 21:17.000
[loud bang]

21:18.120 --> 21:19.160
Easy...

21:21.680 --> 21:23.080
Easy, easy...

21:25.960 --> 21:27.320
Easy, boy.

21:27.400 --> 21:30.320
[shushing]

21:31.680 --> 21:33.760
In France we used to say...

21:34.280 --> 21:38.160
In France we used to say,
it's just the music hall band turning up.

21:38.240 --> 21:40.720
It's just trombones
and tubas, that's all.

21:40.800 --> 21:42.080
It's just noise.

21:45.080 --> 21:48.160
It's just noise.
Hey? It's just noise.

21:49.080 --> 21:52.400
You get used to it.
It's just noise.

21:52.480 --> 21:55.720
Good boy. Good boy. Good boy.

22:07.600 --> 22:09.480
Some pain there, boy?

22:14.800 --> 22:16.640
I'm so sorry, Mr. Shelby.

22:19.360 --> 22:22.080
- I'm Grace, by the way.
- I know who you are.

22:24.400 --> 22:27.760
- What's his name?
- He doesn't have a name.

22:28.440 --> 22:30.320
Poor boy deserves a name.

22:31.360 --> 22:33.240
You have something
to say to me?

22:36.680 --> 22:39.960
The other night you came into the pub
when I was singing.

22:40.560 --> 22:42.480
You said singing wasn't allowed.

22:44.280 --> 22:47.760
I'd like there to be one night a week
when there is singing.

22:49.280 --> 22:51.520
I think it would be good
for everyone.

22:51.600 --> 22:52.960
Saturday nights.

22:55.000 --> 22:57.600
Harry was too afraid
to ask you, so...

22:58.200 --> 22:59.640
But you're not.

23:02.920 --> 23:04.080
I am.

23:05.600 --> 23:07.160
But I love to sing.

23:09.080 --> 23:12.960
You sound like one of those
rich girls who come over from Dublin,

23:13.040 --> 23:15.040
for the races.

23:15.120 --> 23:16.840
Do you like horses?

23:18.840 --> 23:21.280
How do you fancy
earning some extra money?

23:21.360 --> 23:22.640
Doing what?

23:24.960 --> 23:26.640
Dig out a nice dress.

23:27.520 --> 23:29.480
I want to take you to the races.

23:38.520 --> 23:40.560
[man talking indistinctly]

24:12.200 --> 24:13.720
Sorry, Mr. Shelby.

24:21.960 --> 24:23.280
[people chattering]

24:23.360 --> 24:25.360
Hello, Mr. Shelby,
how's it going?

24:25.440 --> 24:27.800
- Hello, Frank, how's the leg?
- Not bad.

24:27.880 --> 24:30.040
Good. Nice to see you, son.

24:31.320 --> 24:32.840
Morning, Charlie.

24:33.040 --> 24:36.840
- All right, Scudboat, how are we looking?
- Looking good, Tommy.

24:36.920 --> 24:38.520
- Grand stuff.
- Tommy,

24:38.600 --> 24:40.600
they are all here
for Monaghan Boy.

24:40.680 --> 24:42.960
That's what I like
to hear, Johnny boy.

24:43.040 --> 24:44.880
And Aunt Pol wants to see you.

24:54.320 --> 24:56.360
[inaudible]

25:37.560 --> 25:38.720
[sighs]

25:48.120 --> 25:50.000
[sighs]

25:50.080 --> 25:52.120
Tell me the man's name, Ada.

25:53.200 --> 25:55.160
Rudolf Valentino.

26:15.120 --> 26:16.560
Get out!

26:16.880 --> 26:19.880
All of you! Go on! Now!

26:23.680 --> 26:25.880
I said, tell me his fucking name.

26:29.640 --> 26:32.440
Freddie fucking Thorne!

26:34.440 --> 26:40.120
Yeah. Your best mate since school.
The man who saved your life in France!

26:40.200 --> 26:45.360
So go on! Go on, cut him!
Cut him up and chuck him in the Cut!

26:48.240 --> 26:53.280
Oye, I'm a Shelby, too, you know.
Put my fucking film back on!

27:26.440 --> 27:29.080
So Monaghan Boy finally lost.

27:30.960 --> 27:32.720
Third time unlucky.

27:33.520 --> 27:35.640
We took money
from all over the city.

27:35.720 --> 27:38.680
Yeah, but you'll pay it back
to people around here.

27:39.200 --> 27:40.760
Buy your popularity back.

27:41.240 --> 27:42.560
Already done.

27:42.960 --> 27:44.400
Taught you well.

27:45.520 --> 27:49.200
And you fixed this race without
the permission of Billy Kimber?

27:50.720 --> 27:53.040
Obviously, didn't teach you
well enough.

27:53.120 --> 27:55.680
Rule one. You don't punch
above your weight.

27:55.760 --> 27:58.800
- Billy Kimber is there for the taking.
- Says who?

27:58.880 --> 28:01.080
Says Tommy
and his parliament of one?

28:01.160 --> 28:03.600
I ran this business
for five years...

28:03.680 --> 28:06.200
Yeah, while I was
away fighting, remember?

28:06.280 --> 28:10.240
I learnt some things, such as, you strike
when your enemy is weak.

28:11.680 --> 28:14.520
I thought you came here
to talk family business.

28:15.720 --> 28:17.040
I'll deal with it.

28:17.120 --> 28:20.160
- You're too busy taking over the world.
- Polly.

28:21.040 --> 28:23.360
If it's about Ada,
I need to know.

28:30.680 --> 28:33.320
Ada wants you to give Freddie this letter.

28:33.960 --> 28:36.760
She wants Freddie to know
she's having his baby.

28:36.840 --> 28:39.680
He deserves an opportunity
to do the right thing.

28:39.960 --> 28:41.800
I say we give them a chance.

28:47.640 --> 28:50.320
For a woman who's had
a hard life with men,

28:50.400 --> 28:52.520
you're still full of romance, eh?

28:53.160 --> 28:55.720
What do you think
Freddie sees in our Ada?

28:55.800 --> 28:57.760
- That's Freddie's business.
- No.

28:57.840 --> 28:59.760
No. I'll tell you what he sees.

28:59.840 --> 29:02.720
He sees machine guns and rifles,

29:02.800 --> 29:05.560
and ammunition
and some glorious revolution.

29:05.640 --> 29:08.400
What is it you really
don't like about Freddie?

29:08.480 --> 29:10.880
She'll have no life
with a man on the run.

29:10.960 --> 29:13.400
If you can't see that,
you can't see much.

29:18.200 --> 29:20.840
Damn them for what they
did to you in France!

29:23.520 --> 29:26.680
Tell Ada, Freddie went to America.

29:27.240 --> 29:28.600
Or Russia.

29:28.680 --> 29:32.880
Polly, it would do no good for Ada
to bring a baby into the world alone.

29:32.960 --> 29:34.240
Pol, listen.

29:34.320 --> 29:37.360
The truth is you would have
hit me with that thing,

29:37.440 --> 29:40.440
if it weren't for the fact
that you know I'm right!

29:44.960 --> 29:46.960
I thought I heard
someone knocking.

29:47.840 --> 29:49.640
Couldn't get back to sleep.

29:53.120 --> 29:57.360
[Polly] The longer you leave it,
the worse it gets.

30:04.760 --> 30:08.120
Believe me, I know.

30:10.720 --> 30:12.480
I was 16.

30:14.760 --> 30:16.800
And I didn't dare tell anyone.

30:17.640 --> 30:19.560
Polly, Freddie will come back.

30:20.200 --> 30:22.240
In the end I did it myself.

30:24.280 --> 30:26.160
I did it to myself.

30:26.800 --> 30:28.480
And I almost died.

30:29.520 --> 30:31.320
And he didn't come back.

30:31.840 --> 30:34.200
They don't.
Why should they?

30:34.880 --> 30:36.480
You know the words.

30:36.560 --> 30:39.600
"You're a whore,
the baby's a bastard."

30:39.680 --> 30:42.920
But there's no word for the man
who doesn't come back.

30:46.240 --> 30:50.080
One day, on your wedding day,

30:50.640 --> 30:53.000
you'll have a good man on your arm,

30:54.000 --> 30:59.160
and you will say,
"Polly, thank you for common sense."

31:08.040 --> 31:09.920
This woman is in Cardiff.

31:11.240 --> 31:13.160
We'll take the train tomorrow.

31:16.600 --> 31:19.160
Go to the castle
afterwards for a treat.

32:12.800 --> 32:17.880
I chose this place because it is outside
both of our jurisdictions.

32:19.800 --> 32:21.440
Do you want tea?

32:23.040 --> 32:25.480
Inspector, I responded
to your invitation,

32:25.560 --> 32:28.040
because I want us to
understand each other.

32:29.000 --> 32:30.840
I am a businessman.

32:31.520 --> 32:33.960
I want to make
my business successful.

32:34.760 --> 32:37.440
And I want my city run peacefully.

32:38.240 --> 32:41.520
If the city is peaceful,
business can thrive.

32:42.880 --> 32:45.280
So we are on the same side?

32:46.440 --> 32:48.320
I think perhaps we could be.

32:55.040 --> 32:56.240
Now...

33:00.160 --> 33:04.080
How can we be on the same side
when I see things like this?

33:06.240 --> 33:12.760
My men found this
in the bedroom of a known communist.

33:13.560 --> 33:15.960
It has your sister's name on it.

33:17.680 --> 33:20.760
It was obvious she had been
sleeping in his bed.

33:21.760 --> 33:26.680
Are you also in bed
with the communists, Mr. Shelby?

33:27.080 --> 33:29.160
I do not share their fantasy.

33:29.960 --> 33:31.640
And as for my sister,

33:31.720 --> 33:34.400
I have already dealt
with the situation.

33:34.880 --> 33:37.480
Freddie Thorne is
at the very top of my list.

33:37.560 --> 33:39.000
Cross him off.

33:39.560 --> 33:41.840
He won't be returning to the city.

33:43.200 --> 33:45.400
I will make him part of our deal.

33:47.000 --> 33:48.480
What deal?

33:52.800 --> 33:56.760
You and your Specials will leave
my businesses alone from now on.

33:56.960 --> 34:00.520
No more raids into our territory,
no more smashing up pubs,

34:00.600 --> 34:02.320
no more lifting my runners.

34:02.400 --> 34:05.520
You turn a blind eye to
all of my gambling operations.

34:05.600 --> 34:06.800
Also...

34:07.440 --> 34:11.160
I am planning an expansion
onto the race tracks.

34:11.280 --> 34:14.360
I intend to do business
with Billy Kimber.

34:14.520 --> 34:18.280
He runs most of the legal
track side betting outside of London.

34:18.440 --> 34:20.640
He has policemen on his payroll.

34:20.720 --> 34:24.920
I want you to put in a word with
the Chief Inspector at Gloucestershire,

34:25.000 --> 34:28.800
that his men should leave me alone
when I make my move.

34:31.840 --> 34:34.200
Forgive me.
I don't seem to have a pen

34:34.280 --> 34:37.400
to write down this rather
long list of demands.

34:40.920 --> 34:42.960
And what do I get in return?

34:44.560 --> 34:46.480
I have what you're looking for.

34:52.040 --> 34:53.480
I have the guns.

34:56.000 --> 34:57.360
What guns?

34:57.440 --> 35:00.040
- I'm not here to play games--
- Wait, wait.

35:11.440 --> 35:16.680
Twenty-five Lewis machine guns.
50 carbines, 10,000 rounds of ammunition.

35:16.760 --> 35:22.200
All in a crate bound for Libya,
stolen from the BSA factory proofing bay.

35:23.080 --> 35:26.800
I'm guessing they sent you to Birmingham
to get those guns back.

35:26.880 --> 35:29.680
Well, it's me that has them.

35:31.800 --> 35:36.000
I have left word with men I trust that
if I am taken into police custody

35:36.080 --> 35:37.360
for whatever reason,

35:37.440 --> 35:39.880
those guns will be shipped to Liverpool.

35:39.960 --> 35:42.840
From there they will be sent directly
to Belfast,

35:42.920 --> 35:45.840
and sold to the Irish Republican Army.

35:46.560 --> 35:49.280
All your good work in Ireland
would be undone.

35:49.360 --> 35:51.920
Each stolen weapon
is numbered and marked.

35:52.000 --> 35:56.400
If I sell them to the IRA, it won't
be long before Mr. Churchill finds out.

35:56.560 --> 36:01.320
I imagine you got into enough trouble over
the burning of the King's photographs.

36:01.400 --> 36:03.080
That was just a taster.

36:03.320 --> 36:09.240
If those guns reach Belfast,
your life in the force is over.

36:10.080 --> 36:13.080
When I have achieved
what I have set out to achieve,

36:13.160 --> 36:15.760
I will let you know
where to find the guns.

36:15.840 --> 36:19.520
You'll be a hero,
you will probably get a medal.

36:22.000 --> 36:23.440
I'm a fair man.

36:24.320 --> 36:25.880
It's a fair offer.

36:28.000 --> 36:29.520
Do we have a deal?

36:38.160 --> 36:39.560
I need an answer.

36:40.160 --> 36:41.440
Right now.

36:46.240 --> 36:47.480
Very well.

36:49.800 --> 36:53.440
But I would prefer
if we don't shake hands on it.

37:06.640 --> 37:09.280
Now why would I
shake the hand of a man,

37:09.360 --> 37:11.600
who didn't even fight
for his country?

37:22.120 --> 37:25.360
[opera music playing]

37:26.680 --> 37:30.360
[singing in foreign language]

37:51.120 --> 37:52.120
[knock at door]

37:55.560 --> 37:56.760
Grace.

38:02.880 --> 38:05.800
I have new information.

38:08.480 --> 38:11.160
Thomas Shelby
is now the beginning,

38:11.240 --> 38:14.320
the middle and end of your mission.

38:26.200 --> 38:27.800
So what shall I do?

38:29.360 --> 38:31.640
It hurts me as much as it,

38:31.720 --> 38:35.760
as it would a father sending his
own daughter into a whore house,

38:38.520 --> 38:41.840
but no matter how repugnant it may be,

38:42.280 --> 38:46.720
you must do everything you can
to get close to him,

38:47.320 --> 38:51.440
and find out where those guns are hidden.

38:54.440 --> 38:57.840
Of course, when I say,
"Everything," I don't mean...

38:59.200 --> 39:02.000
You underestimate me in every way.

39:11.600 --> 39:12.840
Here...

39:23.400 --> 39:28.440
You are now active in a military operation
on behalf of the Crown.

39:30.160 --> 39:34.200
And I wish to God,
circumstance hadn't chosen you.

39:35.880 --> 39:37.640
It's what I am trained for.

39:38.800 --> 39:40.400
I'm late for my shift.

39:44.840 --> 39:45.880
Grace...

39:47.840 --> 39:49.800
My heart is with you.

39:58.400 --> 40:00.640
[dramatic opera music playing]

40:02.560 --> 40:05.920
[singing in foreign language]

40:06.000 --> 40:07.160
[screaming]

40:10.080 --> 40:11.520
[gunshot]

40:25.000 --> 40:26.160
[panting]

40:48.720 --> 40:51.000
Tom! Tom!

40:51.360 --> 40:54.920
Tom, you better come quick, Tom!
Come! Come!

41:22.640 --> 41:25.800
- Curly, tell me.
- It's a curse, Tom.

41:27.600 --> 41:31.400
Curly. Curly. Curly. Shh. Curly. Curly.

41:32.520 --> 41:35.920
Tell me. Tell me, Curly,
what's wrong with the horse?

41:36.000 --> 41:38.520
You bought it at the fair in bad feeling.

41:38.600 --> 41:42.640
The Lees put a bad seed in the hoof.
Got an old woman to put a spell!

41:42.760 --> 41:44.320
[mumbling]

41:47.560 --> 41:49.920
So those Lee bastards cursed her.

41:51.160 --> 41:54.560
Whatever it is, he says
it spread to the other foot.

41:54.640 --> 41:57.600
It's going to his heart
by tomorrow, I'd say.

41:57.680 --> 42:01.120
Seen curses like this twice.
Can't take them back, Tom!

42:01.200 --> 42:02.720
No! No!

42:03.000 --> 42:05.000
I told you, Tommy.

42:05.440 --> 42:08.560
Better enemies to have
than black blood Gypsies.

42:12.080 --> 42:13.360
Get out.

42:14.600 --> 42:16.080
Get out, Curly.

42:22.240 --> 42:23.560
I'm sorry.

42:35.080 --> 42:36.920
[gunshot]

42:52.840 --> 42:54.560
[banging on door]

43:02.600 --> 43:06.520
- We're closed, Mr. Shelby.
- Just get me a drink.

43:13.880 --> 43:15.400
[coughs]

43:20.040 --> 43:21.680
Shall I leave you alone?

43:23.280 --> 43:25.160
I came here for company.

43:26.160 --> 43:29.400
- Where's Harry?
- He took the night off.

43:32.040 --> 43:33.600
And went to the pictures.

43:40.960 --> 43:42.640
How's your beautiful horse?

43:52.480 --> 43:54.520
I just put a bullet in his head.

43:58.680 --> 44:00.040
Was he lame?

44:03.120 --> 44:05.560
He looked at me the wrong way.

44:06.600 --> 44:10.000
It's not a good idea to look
at Tommy Shelby the wrong way.

44:15.240 --> 44:19.480
- What a waste.
- Yeah. A waste is what it is.

44:26.080 --> 44:27.600
You know, in France...

44:32.160 --> 44:35.240
In France, I got used to seeing men die.

44:36.720 --> 44:39.240
Never got used to seeing horses die.

44:41.080 --> 44:42.840
They die badly.

44:49.600 --> 44:51.680
I dug out a dress like you asked.

44:52.920 --> 44:54.040
Thanks.

44:56.480 --> 44:58.680
Is it Cheltenham
you're talking about?

45:05.960 --> 45:08.280
Cheltenham's a grand affair, is it not?

45:08.360 --> 45:10.040
The King will be there.

45:10.120 --> 45:12.520
- King George?
- No.

45:13.600 --> 45:17.160
King Billy Kimber and all his men.

45:19.480 --> 45:21.160
And what must I do?

45:21.240 --> 45:24.080
For two pounds you will do
what I ask you to do.

45:25.680 --> 45:27.120
I want three.

45:28.760 --> 45:32.280
If I'm meeting a king,
I won't be wearing a cheap dress.

45:35.440 --> 45:39.560
And I asked you to let me sing.
That's part of the deal now too.

45:40.600 --> 45:42.040
Since when?

45:44.080 --> 45:45.720
Since you nearly smiled.

45:50.360 --> 45:52.840
Saturday nights.
Open and easy.

45:52.920 --> 45:56.520
Everyone gets to sing their song,
just like we did in Dublin.

45:56.600 --> 46:00.240
You never worked in Dublin,
so don't lie to me.

46:00.320 --> 46:04.160
I asked around about that pub
you said you used to work in.

46:04.240 --> 46:05.960
I have friends over there.

46:06.840 --> 46:08.640
No one has heard of you.

46:15.040 --> 46:16.640
My guess is,

46:17.520 --> 46:21.840
you are a girl from a good family,
who got herself pregnant.

46:24.960 --> 46:26.960
It's not something I want known.

46:27.040 --> 46:30.480
And bringing a child into the world alone
ruined your life.

46:31.480 --> 46:32.760
Right?

46:36.680 --> 46:40.600
So I'm right and Polly is wrong.

46:41.520 --> 46:43.000
Right about what?

46:43.880 --> 46:45.360
It doesn't matter.

46:46.800 --> 46:48.840
It looks like it matters to you.

46:49.400 --> 46:51.240
Family business.

46:54.920 --> 46:56.880
You won't tell anyone my secret.

46:58.040 --> 47:00.400
Do you think I tell people things?

47:04.320 --> 47:05.920
So what do you sing?

47:07.760 --> 47:10.120
- Anything you want.
- Right.

47:11.000 --> 47:12.480
Get up on a chair.

47:13.360 --> 47:14.400
Okay.

47:21.920 --> 47:23.280
Happy or sad?

47:27.000 --> 47:28.080
Sad.

47:29.720 --> 47:30.960
Okay.

47:31.040 --> 47:34.320
But I warn you,
it will break your heart.

47:35.560 --> 47:37.080
Already broken.

47:41.960 --> 47:47.160
♪ In a neat little town 
They call Belfast ♪

47:47.800 --> 47:51.520
♪ Apprentice to trade
I was bound ♪

47:52.520 --> 47:58.080
♪ Many an hour sweet happiness ♪

47:58.440 --> 48:03.080
♪ Had I spent in that neat little town ♪

48:04.480 --> 48:09.960
♪ A sad misfortune came over me ♪

48:10.040 --> 48:14.440
♪ Which caused me to stray from the land ♪

48:16.080 --> 48:23.000
♪ Far away from my friends and relations ♪

48:24.840 --> 48:31.600
♪ Betrayed by the black velvet band ♪

48:32.080 --> 48:36.880
♪ Her eyes, they shone like diamonds ♪

48:37.160 --> 48:41.520
♪ I thought her the queen of the land ♪

48:42.800 --> 48:48.600
♪ And her hair
It hung over her shoulder ♪

48:49.080 --> 48:50.760
♪ Tied up ♪

48:51.920 --> 48:55.240
♪ With a black velvet band ♪

49:15.240 --> 49:17.040
- You okay?
- Yeah, I'm fine.

49:17.840 --> 49:21.920
I think there are some carriages
over there that are gonna be empty...

49:22.480 --> 49:23.560
[Polly] Jesus.

49:24.920 --> 49:26.200
Freddie.

49:27.120 --> 49:28.520
You came back?

49:31.240 --> 49:32.720
I got a tip-off.

49:32.800 --> 49:34.520
Freddie, Tommy will kill you.

49:34.600 --> 49:36.560
It was Tommy
who tipped me off.

49:36.640 --> 49:39.080
Maybe he's got
half a heart after all.

49:39.760 --> 49:41.760
Tommy got a message to me.

49:42.120 --> 49:45.360
It said, "Get out of town,
take her with you."

49:46.920 --> 49:48.000
So...

49:51.640 --> 49:53.280
Ada Shelby...

49:56.120 --> 49:57.840
Will you marry me?

49:59.000 --> 50:00.280
Yes!

50:00.760 --> 50:01.960
Yes.

50:03.840 --> 50:05.040
Yes!

50:20.160 --> 50:23.680
Right, get on that bloody train
and get out of here.

50:23.760 --> 50:26.320
- Come on, both of you.
- Freddie, come on.

50:26.400 --> 50:30.280
Freddie, Cardiff's okay.
Anywhere but here's okay.

50:30.440 --> 50:32.480
We're not going anywhere, Ada.

50:34.440 --> 50:37.600
We are going to stay here,
we are going to marry here.

50:38.640 --> 50:40.880
I'm not afraid of Tommy Shelby.

50:50.800 --> 50:54.560
[all singing "The Sunshine of Your Smile"]

51:03.080 --> 51:05.680
[Arthur] Shuffle these cards and...

51:05.760 --> 51:07.680
Go and play snap with Finn.

51:07.760 --> 51:11.040
- [Grace] Did you want whiskey as well?
- No. Just beer.

51:11.240 --> 51:14.440
Why no whiskey, Tommy?
Are you expecting trouble?

51:16.880 --> 51:20.280
Jesus Christ, Tommy, what the hell
made you let them sing?

51:20.360 --> 51:22.920
It's like they're strangling
cats out there.

51:23.000 --> 51:25.600
[Arthur] All right.
20 is the play. Come on.

51:26.000 --> 51:29.360
And what did make you change your mind,
though, Tommy?

51:30.680 --> 51:31.920
Yeah, it is.

51:32.000 --> 51:34.960
- It's about time, Tommy.
- Time for what?

51:36.040 --> 51:39.920
- Time you took yourself a woman.
- Just play the bloody hands.

51:40.080 --> 51:44.160
You stay the way you are, Tommy.
Remember what Dad used to say.

51:44.240 --> 51:45.960
Fast women and slow horses...

51:46.040 --> 51:47.680
[both] Will ruin your life.

51:47.760 --> 51:48.840
[car approaching]

51:49.040 --> 51:51.800
- [John] Coppers.
- No.

51:54.360 --> 51:57.360
[all singing]

52:13.720 --> 52:17.800
Holy shit. It's Billy Kimber.

52:26.560 --> 52:29.560
Is there any man here
named Shelby?

52:36.680 --> 52:40.760
I said, is there any man here
named Shelby?

52:47.000 --> 52:50.640
Harry, get these men a drink.
Everyone else, go home!

53:03.880 --> 53:05.400
You go home.

53:05.480 --> 53:08.080
- But Mr. Fenton said--
- I said, go home.

53:10.560 --> 53:14.600
I've never approved of women in pubs,
but when they look like that...

53:14.680 --> 53:17.120
You said you wanted men
called Shelby.

53:17.200 --> 53:19.080
You've got three of them.

53:19.480 --> 53:22.480
I'd never heard of you.
Then I did hear of you.

53:22.560 --> 53:26.480
Some little Diddicoy razor gang.
I thought to myself, "So, what?"

53:27.040 --> 53:32.160
But then you fuck me over.
So now you have my undivided attention.

53:33.720 --> 53:36.840
By the way, which one am I talking to?
Who's the boss?

53:37.680 --> 53:39.000
Well, I'm the oldest.

53:39.080 --> 53:41.560
[laughs] Clearly.

53:41.640 --> 53:43.600
Are you laughing at my brother?

53:45.880 --> 53:48.400
Right, he's the oldest,
you're the thickest.

53:48.480 --> 53:52.160
I'm told the boss is called Tommy
and I'm guessing that's you,

53:52.240 --> 53:55.800
'cause you're looking me up and down
like I'm a fucking tart.

53:56.000 --> 53:58.040
I want to know what you want.

53:58.120 --> 54:01.320
There were suspicious
betting patterns at Kempton Park.

54:01.400 --> 54:03.520
A horse called Monaghan Boy.

54:04.080 --> 54:07.120
He won by a length twice
and then finished last,

54:07.200 --> 54:09.560
with 3,000 pounds bet on him.

54:09.640 --> 54:11.360
Which one am I talking to?

54:11.880 --> 54:13.600
Which one of you is the boss?

54:13.680 --> 54:16.040
I am Mr. Kimber's adviser
and accountant.

54:16.120 --> 54:19.200
And I'm the fucking boss.
Okay, right, end of parley.

54:19.280 --> 54:21.800
You fixed a race
without my permission.

54:21.920 --> 54:24.840
You fucking Gypsy scum.

54:24.920 --> 54:28.920
What, live off the war pensions
of these poor Garrison Lane widows!

54:29.000 --> 54:30.240
That's your level!

54:30.320 --> 54:33.880
I am Billy Kimber,
I run the races!

54:33.960 --> 54:37.880
You fixed one of them so I'm going
to have you shot against a post.

54:39.200 --> 54:40.360
Mr. Kimber.

54:44.760 --> 54:46.000
[Thomas] Look at it.

54:47.680 --> 54:49.600
That is my name on it.

54:50.240 --> 54:52.360
It's from the Lee family.

54:53.120 --> 54:56.480
You are also at war with the Lees,
Mr. Kimber, am I right?

54:59.600 --> 55:03.000
The Lees are attacking your bookies
and taking your money.

55:03.080 --> 55:06.760
Your men can't control them.
You need help.

55:07.960 --> 55:11.400
Perhaps we should listen
to what Mr. Shelby has to say.

55:11.640 --> 55:13.760
Before we make our decisions.

55:14.240 --> 55:18.320
Right. The Lees are doing
a lot of talking at the fairs.

55:18.400 --> 55:19.760
They have a lot of kin.

55:19.840 --> 55:22.520
They're saying
the race tracks are easy meat,

55:22.600 --> 55:25.000
because the police
are busy with strikes.

55:25.080 --> 55:28.120
Now, we have connections.
We know how they operate.

55:28.200 --> 55:30.840
You have muscle.

55:30.920 --> 55:34.200
Together, we can beat them.
Divided, maybe not.

55:36.160 --> 55:40.560
Mr. Kimber, perhaps we should
take some time for reflection,

55:40.640 --> 55:43.960
possibly make arrangements
for a second meeting.

55:44.040 --> 55:46.320
I admire you, Mr. Kimber.

55:46.760 --> 55:51.040
You started with nothing,
and built a legitimate business.

55:51.640 --> 55:56.040
It would be an honor
to work with you, Mr. Kimber.

55:57.920 --> 56:02.320
Nobody works with me.
People work for me.

56:07.040 --> 56:08.760
Pick it up, pikey.

56:19.480 --> 56:21.320
Sit, sit down.

56:29.320 --> 56:33.360
- It's for your ceiling.
- Thank you, Mr. Kimber.

56:37.000 --> 56:40.960
- We will be at Cheltenham.
- As will I.

57:01.720 --> 57:04.480
So you picked a fight
with the Lees on purpose.

57:04.560 --> 57:08.600
Tommy, we can't mess
with Billy fucking Kimber.

57:08.680 --> 57:12.760
Get yourself a decent haircut, man.
We're going to the races.

57:18.280 --> 57:21.640
[theme song playing]
